window.docContents[141].resolve({"/Lexer-and-grammar/Preprocessor/#Core-C______--Lexer-and-grammar--Preprocessor":{"contents":"Preproc is the preprocessor of Core C++, a language of its own that runs before the lexer of . It reads a file as a sequence of lines and never lexes or parses Core C++, so the two languages share no surface. Every Preproc program is also a valid input of the preprocessor of g++. The terminals of its grammar are whole lines.  stands for any text line, and  ends a directive line.Preproc supports conditional compilation on flags, the inclusion of a subset of the standard library and of the STL, and the macro assert of <cassert>. A flag has no value and never reaches the code. The design is preproc/ccpp-preproc.md.\n\nEvery line satisfies five rules, the lines of a branch that is not selected and the lines of every header included. The C++ preprocessor splices lines and removes comments before it recognises directives (N4659 §5.2, phases 2 to 4), and the rules keep the line structure the same for both. A directive line is exactly one of the forms of the grammar. A text line does not start with # or with the digraph %:, which C++ reads as # (N4659 §5.5, Table 1). No line contains a backslash, /*, a single quote or a double quote. A flag has the prefix CCPP_ and no __, which C++ reserves (N4659 §5.10). No word of a text line starts with CCPP_, so a flag never occurs in the code.\n\nThe flag environment  is a finite set of flags, initially the flags given as -D CCPP_X=. The judgement  gives the output lines  of a group and the environment after it.  is the contents of the Core C++ header .The directive #ifndef swaps the premises  and , and a missing #else stands for an empty . A text line gives itself. The output keeps one line for each source line outside the headers, an empty one for every directive line and every line of a branch that is not selected.\n\nThe headers of Core C++ are Core C++ code for the Core C++ compiler, in preproc/include, and g++ uses its own headers. Each is a Preproc file whose guard makes it idempotent, as a C++ header is (N4659 §20.5.2.2, paragraph 2). The headers <cassert>, <vector> and <functional> hold only their guard, since assert, std::vector and std::function are built into Core C++. An #include inside a function body puts declarations inside a block, which the grammar of  rejects.\n\nTake a Preproc program  whose output Core C++ accepts. Compiling  with g++ and running the output in Core C++ give the same exit status under the same -D options.\n\nThe preprocessor of C++ selects the same lines as Preproc (N4659 §19.1, paragraphs 11 and 12), and the lexical rules keep flags out of the code. Each Core C++ header agrees with the g++ header of the same name on the names the subset admits, one obligation per header. The file tests/Preproc.lean checks the argument on the programs of tests/preproc, against g++ -E for the selected lines and against g++ for the exit status. No Lean proof covers it.\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009Lexer and grammar","header":"1.4. Preprocessor","id":"/Lexer-and-grammar/Preprocessor/#Core-C______--Lexer-and-grammar--Preprocessor"},"/Storage-and-commands/Local-reference/#Core-C______--Storage-and-commands--Local-reference":{"contents":"The local reference τ& y = e binds a second name to the location e denotes, without allocating. The initialiser must denote a location and have the declared type, and the reference has in  the type of its referent, because every read and write through it is a read or write at the referent. The binding is an alias, not owned, so the exit of the block that declared the reference leaves the location in . A reference to a temporary, to nullptr or to an expression without a location does not exist, and a dangling reference is impossible by construction, because a reference names only locations of enclosing blocks or of objects in the store.\n\n","context":"Core C++\u0009Storage and commands","header":"4.2. Local reference","id":"/Storage-and-commands/Local-reference/#Core-C______--Storage-and-commands--Local-reference"}});